---
/**
 * RO e-Factura, for foreign-owned companies.
 *
 * Not a translation of the Romanian guide. A Romanian reader needs to know
 * the deadlines and the penalties; a foreign owner usually needs something
 * more basic first — that Romania has mandatory clearance e-invoicing at
 * all, that it applies to them on identical terms, and that their group's
 * ERP invoice is not a valid Romanian invoice by itself.
 */
import ServiceLayout from '@layouts/ServiceLayout.astro';
import Tbd from '@components/ui/Tbd.astro';
---

<ServiceLayout service="efactura" serviceType="E-invoicing compliance">
  <p class="lede">
    Romania operates mandatory clearance e-invoicing. Invoices are not sent
    from you to your customer — they are submitted to the tax authority, which
    validates them and makes the cleared version the legally valid one.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you have run a company elsewhere in the EU, this is the part that
    usually surprises: the invoice your group's ERP produces is not a valid
    Romanian invoice on its own, no matter how correct it looks.
  </p>

  <h2>How clearance changes the invoice</h2>

  <p>
    You upload the invoice to SPV, the tax authority's electronic portal.
    ANAF applies an electronic seal, and the sealed document is the invoice
    that counts. A PDF emailed to your customer has no independent fiscal
    standing.
  </p>

  <p>
    The practical consequence: an invoice exists because it was accepted, not
    because you issued it. One rejected and left uncorrected has, fiscally,
    not been issued at all — while your accounts and your customer's may both
    show it as outstanding.
  </p>

  <h2>It applies to you on identical terms</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>All B2B transactions between taxable persons established in Romania</li>
    <li>All B2G — supplies to public institutions</li>
    <li>From <Tbd label="dată intrare B2C în sistem" />, B2C as well</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    There is no size threshold and no exemption for foreign ownership. A
    Romanian subsidiary of a foreign group is subject to exactly the same
    rules as a local company with one shareholder.
  </p>

  <h2>The deduction risk runs in your direction</h2>

  <p>
    Late submission carries a fine from
    <Tbd label="cuantum amendă netransmitere" />, scaled by taxpayer size.
    The fine is rarely the expensive part.
  </p>

  <p>
    An invoice received outside the system gives you <em>no right to deduct
    VAT</em>. So if a Romanian supplier emails you an invoice and never uploads
    it, the loss lands on you at the next inspection, not on them. Chasing
    suppliers who have not filed is a real, recurring task — and one most
    foreign owners do not realise they have acquired.
  </p>

  <h2>You need a Romanian digital certificate</h2>

  <p>
    SPV access requires a qualified digital certificate from an accredited
    Romanian provider, issued to the administrator or to a holder of a power
    of attorney. It costs around
    <Tbd label="cost certificat digital calificat" /> per year and takes
    <Tbd label="durată obținere certificat" /> to obtain.
  </p>

  <p>
    A non-resident administrator can hold one, but the process is slower. In
    most cases it is simpler to grant a power of attorney to your accountant
    in Romania — which is what we do for the foreign-owned companies we work
    with.
  </p>

  <h2>Connecting your existing system</h2>

  <p>
    If your group invoices from SAP, NetSuite, Xero or similar, you do not
    have to abandon it. The Romanian requirement is that the invoice reaches
    SPV in the required XML format and comes back sealed. That can be done by
    export and upload, or by connecting the system to the API.
  </p>

  <p>
    We set this up and then run it: transmission, checking the status of each
    invoice, pulling down what suppliers have filed, and correcting rejections
    within the deadline.
  </p>

  <h2>What we handle</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>SPV enrolment, including obtaining the digital certificate</li>
    <li>Power of attorney so filings can be made on your behalf</li>
    <li>Submitting issued invoices and monitoring each one's status</li>
    <li>Retrieving supplier invoices and reconciling them to the ledger</li>
    <li>Chasing suppliers who have not filed</li>
    <li>Correcting rejected invoices within the deadline</li>
    <li>Reporting to you in English on what was filed and what is outstanding</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    For clients on a monthly
    <a href="/en/services/accounting-srl">accounting retainer</a>, retrieval
    from SPV is included — it is how we obtain your documents in any case.
  </p>

  <h2>Fees</h2>

  <p>
    Enrolment and initial setup: <Tbd label="tarif înrolare SPV" />, one-off.
    Monthly transmission and monitoring: from
    <Tbd label="tarif lunar e-Factura" />, depending on invoice volume.
  </p>
</ServiceLayout>
